{"author":"Rebar Bot","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"fields":{"description":"## Context / Problem\n\n`tests/unit/grounding/test_terraform_corroborator.py::test_pinned_upstream_canary` fails on the\n`optionality / terraform-config-inspect pinned canary` job with\n`AssertionError: assert 'abstain' == 'match'`, on changes that are fully up to date with `main`.\nObserved on Gerrit change 2674, whose base is `0033662b5534` (current `main`), in\n`https://github.com/navapbc/rebar/actions/runs/33961826977/job/101295228786`, and identically on\nthe dependabot PR-merged tree in job `101290520693`. Rebasing does not help. The job runs on every\npatchset, so this blocks the review queue.\n\n`go install` is NOT the cause: its step logs ten successful module downloads and no error, and the\ntest's own `has_binary` guard passed — execution reached the assertion at line 429. The\ndiscriminating three-way test proving this is recorded as a ticket comment.\n\n## Root cause\n\nThe corroborator abstains `config_inspect_schema_skew`. Reproduced locally and deterministically\nagainst the audited binary (`go install\ngithub.com/hashicorp/terraform-config-inspect@2fb54c236733ee65ee877105d595c124c993c64d`):\n\n```\noutcome      : abstain\nreason       : version_skew\nreason_detail: config_inspect_schema_skew\n```\n\n`src/rebar/schemas/terraform_config_inspect.schema.json` is a producer contract that the pinned\ntool's output can never satisfy. Validating one real invocation yields 15 violations in three\nclasses:\n\n1. `pos` requires `column` and `byte`. Upstream `SourcePos` has exactly two fields —\n   `Filename string` and `Line int` (`tfconfig/source_pos.go` at the pinned commit).\n2. The top-level `required` list demands `required_core`, `provider_configs` and `diagnostics`.\n   The tool omits all three when empty, so a clean module never carries them.\n3. `resource.provider` is typed `[\"string\", \"null\"]`. The tool emits an object, `{\"name\": \"aws\"}`.\n\nBehind the schema sits a second, latent abstain. `tfconfig/load.go`'s `dirFiles` builds\n`fullPath := filepath.Join(dir, name)` and hands that to the parser, so `pos.filename` echoes the\ndirectory argument. `_run` passes the ABSOLUTE snapshot path\n(`src/rebar/grounding/terraform_corroborator.py:286`), so filenames come back absolute, and\n`_validate_positions` rejects any absolute filename at\n`src/rebar/grounding/terraform_corroborator.py:367` with `path_outside_snapshot`. Fixing only the\nschema would move the abstain, not remove it. Confirmed by running the tool both ways: an absolute\ndir argument gives `/tmp/realtf/snap/main.tf`; `.` gives `main.tf`.\n\nThe unit suite missed both because it never uses the real tool — `_fixture_binary` builds a fake\nwhose payload was hand-written, carrying `column`/`byte` fields the tool does not emit. The canary\nis the one test that touches reality.\n\nThis is a THIRD, independent defect alongside `persuasive-welcome-nymph` (a wall-clock race in the\nfault test's own fixture) and `ergophilic-freudian-queenbee` (a CI step failing on pre-gate trees).\nSame provenance commit `59d504c2bdbb` / Gerrit 2672, three distinct mechanisms.\n\n## Approach\n\nCorrect the producer contract to the tool that is actually pinned, and stop feeding the tool a\npath it will echo back:\n\n1. In `terraform_config_inspect.schema.json`, make `pos` require only `filename` and `line`\n   (keeping `column`/`byte` as optional properties so a tool revision that adds them still\n   validates), drop `required_core`/`provider_configs`/`diagnostics` from the top-level `required`\n   list while keeping them as optional properties, and type `resource.provider` as the\n   `{name, alias}` object the tool emits, or null.\n2. In `_run`, pass `\".\"` as the directory argument instead of the absolute snapshot path. The\n   child's `cwd` is already the snapshot, so this names the same directory, and it makes the tool\n   stamp relative `pos.filename`s that `_validate_positions` accepts without relaxing that check\n   at all. It also strengthens the existing redaction posture, which already asserts that no argv\n   element contains the repo path.\n3. Update `test_subprocess_contract_strips_environment_and_copies_readonly_snapshot` to assert the\n   new argv contract, and the recorded `invocation.argv` placeholder in `_match_result` to match.\n\nCorrecting the schema rather than loosening the abstain is the right shape: the abstain logic is\nsound (it refuses to emit T1 evidence it cannot ground), and the canary is correct to refuse a\nvacuous pass. The defect is that the contract describes a tool that does not exist.\n\nDe-risked before codifying: with exactly these changes applied in a throwaway harness against the\nreal audited binary, `variable.region` returns MATCH, `variable.missing` returns abstain\n`no_unique_address`, and `module_source_equals` on `module.vpc` returns MATCH — the canary's own\nassertions, satisfied.\n\n## Scope\n\n- `src/rebar/schemas/terraform_config_inspect.schema.json` — align `pos`, the top-level `required`\n  list, and `resource.provider` with the pinned tool's real output.\n- `src/rebar/grounding/terraform_corroborator.py` — pass `\".\"` as the tool's directory argument in\n  `_run`; update the recorded `invocation.argv` placeholder in `_match_result`.\n- `tests/unit/grounding/test_terraform_corroborator.py` — update the subprocess-contract\n  assertions to the new argv, and the fake payload's `pos`/`provider` shape to the tool's real one\n  so the fake stops contradicting the schema it is validated against.\n\n## Testing\n\n- RED, reproduced locally: with the audited binary on `PATH`,\n  `REBAR_TERRAFORM_CONFIG_INSPECT_CANARY=1 pytest -k pinned_upstream_canary` fails\n  `assert 'abstain' == 'match'`, and a direct `corroborate_diagnostic` call reports\n  `reason_detail=config_inspect_schema_skew`.\n- GREEN, live end-to-end against the real boundary: the same canary command passes with the real\n  audited binary installed — the only check that exercises the actual tool.\n- Latent-path proof: after the schema fix alone the corroboration still abstains\n  `path_outside_snapshot`; after the `\".\"` change as well it matches, showing both links were\n  necessary rather than assuming it.\n- Regression: `pytest tests/unit/grounding/ -q` is green and\n  `pytest tests/unit/grounding/test_terraform_corroborator.py -q` reports the same pass/skip\n  counts as before the change.\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n\n- [ ] With the audited binary on `PATH`,\n      `REBAR_TERRAFORM_CONFIG_INSPECT_CANARY=1 pytest tests/unit/grounding/test_terraform_corroborator.py -k pinned_upstream_canary`\n      PASSES; recorded as a ticket comment alongside the pre-fix failure text.\n- [ ] Validating one real `terraform-config-inspect --json .` document against\n      `terraform_config_inspect.schema.json` yields zero violations; verified by a scripted\n      `iter_errors` count of 0 (it is 15 today).\n- [ ] `_run` passes `\".\"` as the directory argument and the child's `cwd` remains the snapshot;\n      verified by the updated subprocess-contract test, which still asserts no argv element\n      contains the repo path.\n- [ ] Applying only the schema change leaves the corroboration abstaining\n      `path_outside_snapshot`, demonstrating the second link was independently necessary;\n      recorded as a ticket comment.\n- [ ] `pytest tests/unit/grounding/ -q` is green and `make verify` is green before the change is\n      pushed to Gerrit."}},"env_id":"eea68b7f-6697-488b-ac52-744e2ed8e96f","event_type":"EDIT","timestamp":1788609574953095164,"uuid":"f337e049-3509-4862-83df-f73b6fa70d10"}